# text = To those indeed who believe that the English nation ,-- at all times a religious and Catholic people , but who even in the days of the Plantagenets were anti-papal ,-- were in any danger of again falling under the yoke of the Pope of Rome in the reign of James the Second , religious liberty was perhaps acceptable , though it took the shape of a discipline which at once anathematized a great portion of the nation , and virtually establishing Puritanism in Ireland , laid the foundation of those mischiefs which are now endangering the empire .
