# text = They were to meet as if no misunderstanding had ever existed between them ; it was specially stipulated by Lord Marney , that there was to be no Apprised of Egremont's impending arrival , Lord Marney was careful to be detained late that day at petty sessions , and entered the room only a few minutes before dinner was announced , where he found Egremont not only with the countess and a young lady who was staying with her , but with additional bail against any ebullition of sentiment in the shape of the Vicar of Marney , and a certain Captain Grouse , who was a kind of aide-de-camp of the earl ; killed birds and carved them ; played billiards with him , and lost ; had indeed every accomplishment that could please woman or ease man ; could sing , dance , draw , make artificial flies , break horses , exercise a supervision over stewards and bailiffs , and make every body comfortable by taking everything on his own shoulders .
