# text = The Bedouin and the Red Indian never lose their presence of mind ; and the wife of a peasant , when you enter her cottage , often greets you with a propriety of mien which favourably contrasts with your reception by some grand dame in some grand assembly , meeting her guests alternately with a caricature of courtesy or an exaggeradon of supercilious self-control said Egremont bowing to Sybil said Sybil said Egremont continued Gerard said Egremont said Egremont said Gerard said Egremont drawing his chair to the table and looking at Sybil , who intimated assent by a nod said Gerard said Egremont , reading the title page on which also was written said Sybil said Sybil said her father said Egremont asked Gerard said Egremont said Gerard said Egremont turning to Sybil she replied replied Sybil said Gerard , coming unintentionally to the relief of Egremont , who was a little embarrassed said Egremont said Gerard said Sybil despondingly said Gerard said Sybil with a sigh said Gerard said Sybil All this time Egremont was sitting at the table , with the book in his hand , gazing fitfully and occasionally with an air of absence on its title-page , whereon was written the name of its owner .
