# text = It seems like business , tho ' very hard to turn a penny by ; but one's neighbours are very kind , and a little chat about the dreadful times always puts me in spirits said Mrs Trotman said Mr Trotman said the widow exclaimed Chaffing Jack interrupting her said Devilsdust as he entered the Cat and Fiddle with Dandy Mick said Mick said Devilsdust in a tone of scorn said Mick said Mr Trotman said Devilsdust said Mick , bowing gallantly to the lady and so saying Mick and Devilsdust ensconced themselves in the bar , while good-hearted Mrs Carey , sipped her glass of gin and water , which she frequently protested was a pool of Bethesda said Devilsdust said Mick said Chaffing Jack , replying to the previous observation of Devilsdust said Mick said Chaffing Jack said Devilsdust with bitter solemnity said Chaffing Jack said Devilsdust said Jack with a puff said Devilsdust said Mr Trotman said Mick , tossing off his tumbler said Devilsdust said Mick said Devilsdust said Mick said Devilsdust said Mrs Trotman said Devilsdust said Mick said Mrs Carey and repeating her thanks and pinning on her shawl , the widow retired , eager to circulate the intelligence said Devilsdust said Mr Trotman said Devilsdust said Chaffing Jack said Devilsdust said Mick said Devilsdust said Mick said Chaffing Jack said Mick said Chaffing Jack solemnly said Mick said Chaffing Jack said Mick said Devilsdust very seriously said Mick said Devilsdust said Chaffing Jack said Devilsdust said Chaffing Jack said Devilsdust said Mick , and tossing his cap into the air he snapped his fingers with delight at the anticipated amusement said Mr Mountchesney , the son-in-law of Lord de Mowbray , to his wife , as he stood before the empty fire-place with his back to the mantelpiece and his hands thrust into the pockets of his coat said Lady Joan in a tone of tender reproach said Lady Joan said Lady Joan mortified said Mr Mountchesney Lady Joan sighed and affected to recur to the opened volume which during this conversation she had held in her hand said Mr Mountchesney Mr Mountchesney exclaimed , and then walked to the window that looking over the park showed at a distance the advance of a very showy equipage .
